{"reply":"In Chapter 3, “A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale,” the Dodo announces, “_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.” Asked who gives them, it points to Alice: “Why, _she_, of course,” the Dodo replies.

On the page, Alice has just handed out comfits, and her own thimble is presented back to her as a prize. That makes a neat little loop in the ceremony. The narration calls the whole thing absurd, while Alice bows and takes the thimble as solemnly as she can.

Maybe that loop is what feels awkward: recognition arrives, but as something that seems to return your own effort to you rather than meet it. With the lunches, you’re organizing and paying the deposit yourself, so perhaps thanks feel a bit like acknowledgment without reciprocity. Does that fit, or is the awkwardness somewhere else for you?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch03-ln0553-0587","source_location":"Chapter 3 — A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale, source lines 553-587","supported_claims":["In Chapter 3, “A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale,” the Dodo announces, “_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.”"],"exact_quote":"“_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.”"},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch03-ln0578-0617","source_location":"Chapter 3 — A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale, source lines 578-617","supported_claims":["Asked who gives them, it points to Alice: “Why, _she_, of course,” the Dodo replies."],"exact_quote":"“Why, _she_, of course,”"},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch03-ln0578-0617","source_location":"Chapter 3 — A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale, source lines 578-617","supported_claims":["On the page, Alice has just handed out comfits, and her own thimble is presented back to her as a prize.","The narration calls the whole thing absurd, while Alice bows and takes the thimble as solemnly as she can."],"exact_quote":null}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

